#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049930 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050245 :topic/math-academy-id 837 :topic/title "Parallel Lines in the Coordinate Plane"}] :topic/id #uuid "1f64bfc1-4120-531e-ba84-356d2ff12489" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175910 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175736 :question/id #uuid "4dd86f6d-270f-5942-b7cf-df53a0bf4ee4" :question/math-academy-id "e-14361" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(0,1)$ and is parallel to the line $y=\\dfrac{2}{3}x + 5$." :question/worked-solution "Since parallel lines have equal slopes, the slope of the new line should be equal to the slope of the line\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{2}{3}x + 5\n$$\n\nThe slope of the line\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{2}{3}x + 5\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nSo, we want a line with the slope\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nthat passes through the point $(0,1)$. Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= m(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - 1 &= \\frac{2}{3}(x - 0) \\\\\ny - 1 &= \\frac{2}{3}x \\\\\ny &= \\frac{2}{3}x + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Line Passing Through a Point"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175911 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175738 :question/id #uuid "8d4fd310-7de4-5498-9473-68c1a61fde46" :question/math-academy-id "e-14368" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(1,-1)$ and is parallel to the line passing through the points $(3,1)$ and $(6,2)$." :question/worked-solution "Since parallel lines have equal slopes, both lines should have the same slope.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope of the line passing through the points $(3,1)$ and $(6,2)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 - 1}{6 - 3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we wish to find a line that has a slope of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nand passes through the point $(1,-1)$. To find the equation of this line, we use the point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= m(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - (-1) &= \\frac{1}{3}(x - 1) \\\\\ny + 1 &= \\frac{1}{3}x - \\frac{1}{3} \\\\\ny &= \\frac{1}{3}x - \\frac{4}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Line Passing Through Two Points"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175912 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175740 :question/id #uuid "1e0c95a6-24fc-55d1-9c4c-bf87877b3723" :question/math-academy-id "e-14729" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(1,-1)$ and is parallel to the line passing through the points $(2,1)$ and $(6,4)$. Write your answer in **standard form**." :question/worked-solution "Since parallel lines have equal slopes, both lines should have the same slope.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope of the line passing through the points $(2,1)$ and $(6,4)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 - 1}{6 - 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we wish to find a line that has a slope of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nand passes through the point $(1,-1)$. To find the equation of this line, we use point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= m(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - (-1) &= \\frac{3}{4}(x - 1) \\\\\ny + 1 &= \\frac{3}{4}x - \\frac{3}{4} \\\\\ny &= \\frac{3}{4}x - \\frac{7}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write this equation in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= \\frac{3}{4}x - \\frac{7}{4} \\\\\n4y &= 3x - 7 \\\\\n- 3x + 4y &= -7 \\\\\n3x - 4y &= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Working With Lines in Standard Form"}] :topic/math-academy-id 3556 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050586 :topic/math-academy-id 6081 :topic/title "Parallel Lines"}] :topic/title "Finding Equations of Parallel Lines"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050332 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049084 :topic/math-academy-id 2030 :topic/title "Double Integrals in Plane Polar Coordinates"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051070 :topic/math-academy-id 2116 :topic/title "Introduction to Green's Theorem"}] :topic/id #uuid "40868547-c38a-58e8-b266-21013ff254f9" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175987 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175838 :question/id #uuid "11d9077c-a0ac-5d42-9616-b8fe867d1db2" :question/math-academy-id "e-9076" :question/problem "![](images/ff/ffaf242238a8d32ce0f134cff6e0cf4256b2888b0a71f65e3c5ec470cbce284a.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above and the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \\left\\langle 2y + \\ln{(\\sqrt{x+5})}, \\: e^{y/3} + 3x \\right\\rangle.$ \nFind a repeated integral in plane polar coordinates that's equivalent to $\\displaystyle \\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r} \\, .$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}}\n=\n\\oint\\limits_{C} (2y + \\ln{(\\sqrt{x+5})}) \\: \\mathrm{d}x + \\left( e^{y/3} + 3x \\right) \\: \\mathrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y \n= \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/e4/e4c7913793a4b08916a832e575c5b2c167d5dfeec244eea23857731df1e514e7.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)= 2y + \\ln{(\\sqrt{x+5})}$ and $Q(x,y)=e^{y/3} + 3x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(2y+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{x+5}))\\,dx+({e}^{y/3}+3x)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3-2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 3, \\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Line Integral Along a Path as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176023 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175878 :question/id #uuid "1ee74b2f-adff-5c38-8b68-753b1c02f91c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9550" :question/problem "![](images/e1/e12a47b603d2c4193453d0f4daf18f54e52ed973498f520feef32cfafbe28a9f.png)\n\n  \n\nEvaluate ![](images/f1/f16fa431ecef10f5538168a49f4f3ceb4ec827114cab6854142fc9ca4d9ce9af.svg) where the path ![](images/c0/c0c348883f19c18a48444415da35aeaa27f8a18606bf5955e34aec416e248cad.svg) is shown above." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n![](images/ac/accf185e064f4c9c99cc6ad1437aabc7d5471cc332ff6c38d51a3f11ec6e26f2.svg)\n\nIn our case, the region ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) and its boundary ![](images/c0/c0c348883f19c18a48444415da35aeaa27f8a18606bf5955e34aec416e248cad.svg) satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem.\n\n![](images/67/6706cc6b412a98761f0f3cd29e584f1705fa8a708a926b805a8b5702a4f508e8.png)\n\nNow, since\n![](images/8c/8cbe1833f3c6f1612d69aecc8354e66cbf24ef426d648ed5c90224bbcf5ab341.svg)\nwe obtain\n\n![](images/01/01e3a66875647bfc9704bc6a1066dbc18c5cf1c0b0144aff8eb2e478685f83d2.svg)\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n![](images/49/493f02d8a665f5b2dedf5ab543475a28b82bc393d3290a60250d26fbb8065b01.svg)\n\nNow, notice that the region ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n![](images/2c/2c2141d090d129fde56fdfd334e4162927db247703b7ecd62eb14261340ac929.svg)\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n![](images/19/19dfe81d2ed118fd87e4e0ac483119e68f4bbf5ec67ccbaf2614360015ff193a.svg)\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n![](images/0c/0c7d035d915ae4a4cc993d476824897130b85797f32ad753f86e1accd1d5e29e.svg)"} :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Line Integral Using Green's Theorem"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176024 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175879 :question/id #uuid "40a301b7-9eaa-59e1-bcb0-4a35fecbff69" :question/math-academy-id "e-9551" :question/problem "Given that\n![](images/1e/1e78bf74df5607a1d80b3bd51b3222505e3bb201817448a7a5ebe5b0a73cffa9.svg)\nwhere $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \\left\\langle y^2+\\sin x, \\: 3xy-\\cos y  \\right\\rangle$ and $C$ is the circle $x^2+y^2=4$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}}\n=\n\\oint\\limits_{C} (y^2+\\sin x) \\: \\mathrm{d}x + (3xy-\\cos y) \\: \\mathrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y \n= \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/79/798665574b8f0e11d991bb0d934efd01bdeb65840c2e86498be4edbc59578439.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)= y^2+\\sin x$ and $Q(x,y)= 3xy-\\cos y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,dx+(xy-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3y-2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0\\leq r \\leq 2,\\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi  \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is $r^2\\sin\\theta.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Line Integral Along a Circular Path as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem"}] :topic/math-academy-id 3627 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050560 :topic/math-academy-id 2117 :topic/title "Extending Green's Theorem"}] :topic/title "Green's Theorem in Polar Coordinates"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050694 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049459 :topic/math-academy-id 2131 :topic/title "The Divergence of a Vector Field"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050793 :topic/math-academy-id 3716 :topic/title "Calculating Flux in Two-Dimensional Vector Fields"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051070 :topic/math-academy-id 2116 :topic/title "Introduction to Green's Theorem"}] :topic/id #uuid "5f79a517-5af5-5b02-b387-db32653ca27a" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172099 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171905 :question/id #uuid "1c03777d-2ead-5333-bf75-0e9a96fdb0e3" :question/math-academy-id "e-9563" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \\left\\langle -3xy^2 , \\: y+\\cos x \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the triangle with vertices $(0,0), (2,2),$ and $(-2,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\textrm d s = \\iint\\limits_D \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F\\,\\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C P \\,\\mathrm{d}y - Q \\, \\mathrm{d}x = \\iint\\limits_D \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} \\right) \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/aa/aa822f0b9259d6d9eb5c6f439588e70d4b9bee16b2b868fae36c7cbac72d3c3f.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y) = -3xy^2$ and $Q(x,y) = y+\\cos x .$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) :  -y \\leq x \\leq y, \\, 0 \\leq y \\leq 2 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-3{y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-y}^{y}(-3{y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(-3{y}^{2}+1)[x]_{-y}^{y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(-3{y}^{2}+1)(y-(-y))\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(-3{y}^{2}+1)\\cdot 2y\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(-6{y}^{3}+2y)\\,dy \\\\  & =[-6\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}+2\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =[-\\frac{3{y}^{4}}{2}+{y}^{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(-24+4)-0 \\\\  & =-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across Other Type I and Type II Regions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172100 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171906 :question/id #uuid "a9d748d6-d688-5b77-a250-cb534a371765" :question/math-academy-id "e-9564" :question/problem "Consider the rectangle $C$ with vertices $(-1,0), (-1,-3), (1,-3),$ and $(1,0),$  traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the flux of the vector field\n$\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = (7x^2y^6 +x^3)\\, \\mathbf{i} + (- 2xy^7+4x^2y) \\, \\mathbf{j}$\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\textrm d s = \\iint\\limits_D \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F\\,\\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C P \\,\\mathrm{d}y - Q \\, \\mathrm{d}x = \\iint\\limits_D \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} \\right) \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/02/02964b07e45daba2d560fa16d34bcc92ddb8d0e0e83f644f8284dbe9d4b4557b.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y) =  7x^2y^6 +x^3$ and $Q(x,y) =- 2xy^7+4x^2y.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=14x{y}^{6}+3{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-14x{y}^{6}+4{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD = \\{(x,y) :  -1\\leq x\\leq 1, \\, -3\\leq y \\leq 0\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(14x{y}^{6}+3{x}^{2})+(-14x{y}^{6}+4{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-3}^{0}7{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem in Flux Form"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172101 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171907 :question/id #uuid "ed1dfb37-d01e-5d35-82f2-a441cdd54f3e" :question/math-academy-id "e-9565" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \\left\\langle \\sin(\\pi y) - x , \\: \\cos(\\pi x) + 2xy \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the square with vertices $(0,-1), (2,-1), (2,1),$ and $(0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\textrm d s = \\iint\\limits_D \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F\\,\\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C P \\,\\mathrm{d}y - Q \\, \\mathrm{d}x = \\iint\\limits_D \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} \\right) \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/2f/2f3374de4a29c62587a79f6286dfe788e64c37e09e9d9b83b3a67b4dac10dc21.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y) = \\sin(\\pi y) - x$ and $Q(x,y) = \\cos(\\pi x) + 2xy.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) :  0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\, -1 \\leq y \\leq 1 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1+2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x-1)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}-x)\\,{|}_{0}^{2}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({2}^{2}-2)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}2\\,dy \\\\  & =2y\\,{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =2(1)-2(-1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across Rectangles"}] :topic/math-academy-id 3361 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050534 :topic/math-academy-id 2135 :topic/title "The Divergence Theorem"}] :topic/title "Green's Theorem in Flux Form"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050793 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048376 :topic/math-academy-id 3715 :topic/title "Flux in Two-Dimensional Vector Fields"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048540 :topic/math-academy-id 3703 :topic/title "Sums of Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y"}] :topic/id #uuid "6768fffe-a644-5bd7-b35c-6b2cc2563786" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179744 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179606 :question/id #uuid "c1924df7-205f-512e-b024-72f5a654f062" :question/math-academy-id "e-9560" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = (y - x)\\,\\mathbf{i} - (x + y )\\,\\mathbf{j}$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf r(t) = 4\\cos t\\,\\mathbf{i} + 4\\sin t\\,\\mathbf{j}$ for $t \\in [0,2\\pi).$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\oint\\limits_{C}P\\,\\textrm d y - Q\\,\\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = 4\\cos t, \\qquad\n\ty = 4\\sin t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =y-x=4(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t), \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-(x+y)=-4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(4(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t))\\cdot (4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(-4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t))\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{cos}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-16t{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-16(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =-32\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Closed Curve in Parametric Form"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179745 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179607 :question/id #uuid "e9d8e6c2-d346-52fb-918a-5d5c95cd986c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9561" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = xy\\,\\mathbf{i} + x^2\\,\\mathbf{j}$ across the ellipse $C$ with equation $9x^2 + y^2 = 9.$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\oint\\limits_{C}P\\,\\textrm d y - Q\\,\\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nFirst, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{1^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{3^2} = 1.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $1$ and $3,$ respectively. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\cos{t}\\,\\mathbf{i} +3\\sin{t}\\,\\mathbf{j},\n\\qquad 0 \\leq t \\leq 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = \\cos t, \\qquad\ny = 3\\sin t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =xy=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & ={x}^{2}={cos}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $u=\\cos t, \\textrm d u = -\\sin t\\,\\textrm d t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-({cos}^{2}⁡t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{1}^{1}10{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Closed Curve in Cartesian Form"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179746 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179608 :question/id #uuid "337d9d7e-5488-5e6d-af34-e01d22d3d0b9" :question/math-academy-id "e-9574" :question/problem "![](images/6e/6eebbc5ae9f548f5b00de9a12d454fa788884bf73ad46a1065eab0d26330a921.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is given by $y = \\dfrac14x^2$ from the point $\\left(0,0\\right)$ to the point $\\left(4,4\\right).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = -2\\,\\mathbf{i} + x\\,\\mathbf{j}$ through $C,$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$ on $C,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C}P\\,\\textrm d y - Q\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$ *must* be a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(t).$\n\nParameterizing the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = t, \\qquad\ny = \\dfrac{t^2}{4},\n\\qquad\nt\\in \\left[0,4\\right].\n$$\n\n \n\nThis parameterization traverses the curve from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(4,4).$ Therefore, $\\mathbf n$ is indeed a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation of $\\mathbf T,$ so we can apply the above formula for the flux. \n\n![](images/2b/2b94e24344f600fc02ec5ecac6c1e0ee4c6a5450132753a0a5ac5fceaa9aa1fc.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =-2, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =x=t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4})=\\frac{t}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}(-2)\\cdot (\\frac{t}{2})-(t)\\cdot (1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}-2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{t}^{2}{|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =-16-0 \\\\  & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Non-Closed Curve"}] :topic/math-academy-id 3716 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050224 :topic/math-academy-id 4160 :topic/title "Source-Free Vector Fields"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050694 :topic/math-academy-id 3361 :topic/title "Green's Theorem in Flux Form"}] :topic/title "Calculating Flux in Two-Dimensional Vector Fields"}]}
